From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FramePlayer contributors <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] gpu: don't double-close the dmabuf fd of a failed
 Vulkan import

Playing HEVC through the V4L2 hardware decoder (patch 0014) crashes the GPU
process 1-2 minutes in, then the page's renderer with SIGSEGV
(XRWebGLDrawingBuffer::BindAndResolveDestinationFramebuffer, after the GPU
context is lost):

  FATAL scoped_file.cc:45 Check failed: 0 == ret. : Bad file descriptor (9)
  ScopedFDCloseTraits::Free <- NativePixmapHandle::~NativePixmapHandle
    <- VulkanImage::InitializeFromGpuMemoryBufferHandle

strace of the GPU process (close/dup only) at the abort: the decoder thread
dups the frame's two plane fds (192->143, 194->156), then the GPU main thread
closes 143 (by the driver, inside the import), 156 and 143 again (EBADF).
Every earlier frame closes 143 and 156 once each.

VulkanImage::InitializeFromGpuMemoryBufferHandle releases plane 0's fd from
the handle and passes it to vkAllocateMemory, and takes it back
(scoped_fd.reset) if the result is kFailedBeforeAllocateMemory. That result
also covers vkAllocateMemory itself failing, and then the driver has already
closed the fd, so the handle closes a dead fd number.

Give Vulkan a dup and leave the original with the handle, which closes it
once. A failed import may leak the dup (the driver may or may not have closed
it); that beats aborting the GPU process.

Not run on a Frame yet: it needs a Chromium rebuild.
---
diff --git a/gpu/vulkan/vulkan_image_linux.cc b/gpu/vulkan/vulkan_image_linux.cc
index 2ca3580..9fb5346 100644
--- a/gpu/vulkan/vulkan_image_linux.cc
+++ b/gpu/vulkan/vulkan_image_linux.cc
@@ -4,10 +4,13 @@
 
 #include "gpu/vulkan/vulkan_image.h"
 
+#include <unistd.h>
+
 #include <tuple>
 #include <vector>
 
 #include "base/logging.h"
+#include "base/posix/eintr_wrapper.h"
 #include "gpu/vulkan/vulkan_device_queue.h"
 #include "gpu/vulkan/vulkan_function_pointers.h"
 #include "gpu/vulkan/vulkan_util.h"
@@ -87,7 +90,18 @@ bool VulkanImage::InitializeFromGpuMemoryBufferHandle(
     external_image_create_info.pNext = &modifier_info;
   }
 
-  int memory_fd = scoped_fd.release();
+  // vkAllocateMemory() owns the imported fd from the moment it is called, and
+  // a driver may close it even when the allocation then fails (seen with
+  // Turnip importing the Steam Frame's V4L2 NV12 frames). Give Vulkan a
+  // duplicate and leave the original with |native_pixmap_handle|, which closes
+  // it exactly once. Taking the fd back from a failed import (below, before)
+  // closed an fd the driver had already closed, and ScopedFD CHECKs that
+  // close() succeeds: the GPU process aborted.
+  int memory_fd = HANDLE_EINTR(dup(scoped_fd.get()));
+  if (memory_fd < 0) {
+    DPLOG(ERROR) << "Failed to duplicate the GpuMemoryBuffer fd";
+    return false;
+  }
   VkImportMemoryFdInfoKHR import_memory_fd_info = {
       .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_IMPORT_MEMORY_FD_INFO_KHR,
       .handleType = VK_EXTERNAL_MEMORY_HANDLE_TYPE_DMA_BUF_BIT_EXT,
@@ -114,12 +128,10 @@ bool VulkanImage::InitializeFromGpuMemoryBufferHandle(
   auto result = InitializeSingleOrJointPlanes(
       device_queue, size, format, usage, flags, image_tiling,
       &external_image_create_info, &import_memory_fd_info, requirements);
-  // If vkAllocateMemory() returned successfully, the fd in scoped_fd should be
-  // owned by vulkan, otherwise take the ownership of the fd back.
-  if (result == kFailedBeforeAllocateMemory) {
-    scoped_fd.reset(memory_fd);
-  }
-
+  // |memory_fd| is Vulkan's now. If the import failed, the driver may or may
+  // not have closed it, and there is no telling which, so it is left alone: at
+  // worst one fd leaks per failed import, instead of the GPU process aborting
+  // on a double close.
   return result;
 }
 
